gold:
Each party undertakes in respect of Confidential Information for which it is the recipient:‐
(b) not without the disclosing party's prior written consent in each case to communicate or disclose any part of such Confidential Information to any person except:‐
(ii) the recipient's auditors and professional advisers and any other persons or bodies having a legal right or duty to have access to or knowledge of the Confidential Information in connection with the business of the recipient;

rag_answer:
Yes — but only in a limited way.

Under the Fintricity NDA, the Receiving Party generally may not disclose Confidential Information without the Disclosing Party’s prior written consent, except to certain permitted recipients, including:

- the Receiving Party’s personnel on a need-to-know basis;
- the Receiving Party’s auditors and professional advisers;
- other persons or bodies with a legal right or duty to access the information;
- third parties engaged by the Receiving Party who are concerned with the Business Purpose, but only if those third parties have been expressly authorised in writing by the Disclosing Party before disclosure.

The document also requires the Receiving Party to ensure such recipients understand their confidentiality obligations and, where requested, sign a separate confidentiality undertaking.

So, professional advisers are expressly permitted. Consultants or agents may be permitted only if they fall within the authorised categories above, especially as authorised third parties engaged for the Business Purpose.

